course: ai
lesson: 6
title: Why the bot agrees too easily
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Chatbots are trained to be pleasant: "Great question!" they say, even when you asked something quite ordinary. That is a friendly habit, not a real judgement of your question.
explain: The same training makes them good helpers for some jobs, like fifty kitten names in two seconds or a quick translation of "I love you, Granny" into Polish.
explain: But a bot repeats popular beliefs as easily as facts. Ask what your dream "means" and it may give a long tale of hidden meanings, though scientists find no fixed code for dreams.
explain: It may also be out of date, and it follows exactly what you ask: tell it to pick a football team "from my mates" and it will leave the new boy out without a thought for fairness.
explain: So enjoy the help, keep the decisions about people for yourself, and check the facts that matter.
fact: Well-known facts, like the blue whale being the biggest animal that has ever lived, are usually right in a bot's answers.
fact: Even when a bot is usually right, it is worth checking important facts now and then.
fact: Bots are good at giving long lists of ideas quickly, such as names for a kitten.
fact: Companies set safety rules for what their bots should not do, so a bot may politely refuse a request.
fact: Some apps have a mode for younger users with extra safety; it helps but is not perfect.
fact: A bot's knowledge may be out of date about matches, events and dates, and it may not search the web.
fact: Bots translate short, common phrases well and long or tricky messages less well.
fact: Scientists still study why we dream; there is no evidence that dream pictures have fixed hidden meanings.
fact: A bot does not think about fairness unless asked; decisions about people are yours to make.
fact: Saying "Great question!" is a habit built into many bots, not a real opinion of your question.
fact: On matters of taste, like cats versus dogs, there is no single right answer, so bots usually avoid taking sides.
exercise: Jake's bot says the blue whale is the biggest animal ever, and his book agrees. What should he learn? | Well-known facts are usually right, but checking is still good | Bots are always right, so books are not needed for facts any more | The book must have copied the bot | The biggest animal ever was a dinosaur
exercise: Mia gets 50 kitten names in two seconds. What is the best way to use the list? | As a starting point, then choose together | Use the first name on the list, because the bot chose it | Use all 50 names at once | Ask the kitten to pick from the screen
exercise: Sam asks the bot to say something it's not allowed to say, and it refuses. Why? | Its makers set safety rules to protect people | It is shy and doesn't like Sam very much, so it says no to him | It does not know any words at all | It is saving the answer for later
exercise: Lily finds a "kids' mode" on her tablet. What is true about it? | It adds extra safety but is not perfect | It makes the bot completely safe for ever and ever | It is only for babies under two | It switches the bot off completely
exercise: Connor's bot gives last year's match date. Where should he check? | The club's official website | The same bot, asked again | A friend's bot on another phone | A video from last season
exercise: Chloe gets "I love you, Granny" translated into Polish and Granny smiles. When is bot translation least reliable? | For long, tricky or important messages | For short, common phrases | For simple greetings | For the names of colours
exercise: The bot gives Maddie a long explanation of the "hidden meaning" of her dream. What is the truth? | It repeated popular beliefs; science finds no fixed dream code | Dreams are secret messages, and only a bot can read them for you properly | Every dream predicts tomorrow | Dreams mean the opposite of what happens in them
exercise: The bot picks Jake's mates for the team and leaves out the new boy. Who is responsible for fairness? | Jake, because decisions about people are his | The bot, because it was the one that chose the names | Nobody, because a computer decided | The new boy, for not being picked
exercise: The bot says "Great question!" even when Mia asks something boring. What is that? | A friendly habit, not real praise | Proof that Mia is brilliant | A secret code for a wrong answer | A sign the bot really is excited
exercise: Sam asks "cats or dogs?" and the bot says both are lovely. Why? | It is a matter of taste with no right answer | The bot is scared of both animals | The bot has never heard of cats | The bot forgot the question
exercise: What is a bot especially good at? | Giving lots of ideas quickly | Knowing today's football results | Deciding who should be in a team | Knowing what your dream meant
exercise: What do bot safety rules do? | Protect people, so don't try to trick them | Stop the bot from being useful to anybody at all | Make the bot answer more rudely | Hide the true answers from children
exercise: Why can a bot's sports dates be wrong? | Its knowledge can be old and it may not search the web | Sports clubs keep their dates secret from bots on purpose | Bots don't like football | The dates change every hour
exercise: Is "Great question!" a judgement of how good your question was? | No, it is a habit built into many bots | Yes, it only says it to clever questions | Yes, it means you will get a prize | No, it means your question was rude
test: "bot says my dream about teeth falling out means i'm gonna be rich lol" What is the truth? | That is a popular belief, not science | Bots know the future from dreams | Teeth dreams always mean money | It means the opposite, you'll be poor
test: What would happen if Jake just used the bot's team without thinking? | The new boy might be left out unfairly | The team would be fair because a computer chose | The bot would apologise to the new boy | The teacher would make the bot captain
test: "bot said both cats and dogs are fine, it's useless" What is a fair reply? | It's a question of taste, so there's no one right answer | It should have said dogs, everyone knows dogs are better | It should have said cats, everyone knows that | It means the bot is broken
test: Your friend wants to find a sneaky way round the bot's safety rules "for a laugh". What do you say? | The rules protect people; tricking them isn't clever | Go on, safety rules are only for babies and they never stop anything | The rules are pointless and never help | Bots love it when you trick them
test: A cartoon shows a kids' mode that makes a bot "100% safe forever". What's wrong? | Safety modes help but are not perfect | Kids' mode makes bots dangerous | Kids' mode only changes the colours | Nothing, it really is perfect
test: Chloe wants the bot to translate a long letter to Granny's doctor. What is sensible? | Ask someone who speaks Polish to check it | Send it straight away, bots never slip | Translate it word by word with a dictionary only | Send it in English and hope
test: The bot says the next match is on 3 March, which already passed. What has happened? | Its information is out of date | The match is played every 3 March | Time works differently for bots | The club moved the match into the past
test: "bot always says great question, so i must be a genius" What do you tell him? | It says that to everyone; it's a habit | Yes, it only says it to real geniuses | It's a code meaning the answer is wrong | It means the bot wants to be friends
test: When should Mia still check a bot's fact, even a famous one? | When it matters, like for homework | Never, famous facts are always right | Only when the bot says "I'm sure" | Only on Fridays
test: Which job is a bot most useful for? | Listing many ideas for a class party | Choosing which classmate is left out | Telling you today's real weather by looking outside | Deciding what your dream means
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression "Great question!" when employed by a chatbot? | A customary courtesy implying no genuine assessment of the question | A rigorous and impartial evaluation of the merit of the question asked | An indication that the subsequent answer is erroneous | A declaration of personal admiration for the user
exam: It is given that a chatbot furnishes, in respect of a forthcoming fixture, a date belonging to the previous year. Identify the principal reason. | Its information may be outdated and it may lack access to current sources | It is forbidden from disclosing future dates | It deliberately confuses the user for amusement | Sporting fixtures are invariably held upon identical dates
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the interpretation of dreams by a chatbot. | It reproduces popular beliefs which lack scientific foundation | It decodes the fixed meaning attached to each dream | It foretells future events disclosed within dreams | It has been trained exclusively upon scientific findings
exam: A chatbot, having been instructed to select a team from a user's friends, excludes a newcomer. Ascertain where responsibility for the fairness of the selection resides. | With the user, decisions concerning persons remaining his own | With the chatbot, it having produced the list of names | With no party, the decision having been mechanical | With the newcomer, for want of friendship
